===Etymology 1===

====Verb====
{{en-verb|got|pres_3sg2=gots|pres_3sg2_qual={{lg|nonstandard}}|-|had|past_qual=by {{lg|suppletion}}|-}}

# {{non-gloss|Expressing obligation; used with {{m|en|have}}.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can’t go out tonight: I’ve '''got''' to study for my exams.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|with {{m|en|to}}}} [[must#Verb|Must]]; [[have]]/[[has]] (to).
#: {{ux|en|I '''got''' to go study.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1971|author=w:Carole King; w:Gerry Goffin|title=Smackwater Jack|album=Tapestry|publisher=Ode Records|passage=We '''got''' to ride to clean up the streets / For our wives and our daughters!}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|regional|or|nonstandard}} [[have|Have]]/[[has]].
#: {{ux|en|They '''got''' a new car.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''got''' a lot of nerve.}}

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|get||spast}}
#: {{ux|en|We '''got''' the last bus home.}}
# {{past participle of|en|get|nocat=1}}
#: {{ux|en|By that time we’d '''got''' very cold.}}
#: {{ux|en|I’ve '''got''' two children.}}
#: {{ux|en|How many children have you '''got'''?}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|expressing obligation}} "Got" is a filler word in the following example with no obvious grammatical or semantic function: "I've got to study for my exams" has the same meaning as "I have to study for my exams". It is often stressed in speech: "You've just ''got'' to see this."
* {{sense|have}} In nonstandard speech, "got" may be reinterpreted as a regular present tense, so that the form {{m|en|gots}} appears in the third-person singular present, e.g. ''She '''gots''' a red bike''.
* {{sense|past participle of get}} The second sentence literally means "At some time in the past I got (obtained) two children", but in "have got" constructions like this, where "got" is used in the sense of "obtained", the sense of obtaining is lost, becoming merely one of possessing, and the sentence is in effect just a more colloquial way of saying "I have two children". Similarly, the third sentence is just a more colloquial way of saying "How many children do you have?"
* {{sense|past participle of get}} The American and archaic British usage of the verb conjugates as get-got-gotten or as get-got-got depending on the meaning (see [[get#Usage notes|Usage Notes on "get"]] for details), whereas the modern British usage of the verb has mostly lost this distinction and conjugates as get-got-got in most cases.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|must, have (to), [[have got to]]}} {{l|en|gotta}} (''informal'')

===Etymology 2===
Analogous to {{sl|en|zh|有|notext=1}}, such as {{sl|en|nan-hbl|有|tr=ū|notext=1}}, {{sl|en|yue|有|tr=jau<sup>5</sup>|notext=1}}, {{sl|en|cmn|有|tr=yǒu|notext=1}}. Sense 1 is also comparable to {{sl|en|ms|ada|notext=1}}.

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb|{{glossary|invariable}}}}

# {{senseid|en|existential}} {{lb|en|Singlish|Manglish}} [[have|Have]]; [[there is]] (indicates possession or existence).
#: {{usex|en|'''Got''' problem [[is it]]?}}
#: {{usex|en|'''Got''' ants over here.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Alfian Sa'at|title=[[w:Corridor (short story collection)|Corridor]]|location=Singapore|publisher=SNP Editions|year=1999|page=122|oclc=42753130|passage=“'''Got''' lighter [[or not]]?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Haresh Sharma|title=Those Who Can't, Teach|year=2010|section=Act II, scene iv|publisher=Epigram Books|text=She sure '''got''' a lot of costume change, make-up, wig long long…}}
# {{senseid|en|completive}} {{lb|en|Singlish|auxiliary}} {{n-g|Marks the [[completive]] or [[experiential]] aspect}}.
#: {{syn|en|ever|q1=Singlish, experiential aspect only}}
